      "text": "We are given that when Ray climbs the stairs in 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This means that the number of steps n is congruent 1 modulo 4. Thus, we can write n as:\n\nn = 4k + 1  [1]\n\nSimilarly, when Ray climbs the stairs in 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This means that the number of steps n is congruent 2 modulo 5. Thus, we can write n as:\n\nn = 5k + 2  [2]\n\nWe are also given that n > 10 and we want to find the smallest possible value of n that satisfies both [1] and [2]. We can substitute [1] into [2] like so:\n\n4k + 1 = 5k + 2\n\nRearranging terms: \n\nk = 1\n\nSubstituting k = 1 into [1]:\n\nn = 4(1) + 1 = 5  [through this point]\n\nSimilarly, substituting k = 1 into [2]:\n\nn = 5(1) + 2 = 7\n\nSince n > 10, the smallest possible value of n greater than 10 is 11 + n (where n = 4k + 1). This yields:\n\nn = 7 + 4(4) = 23\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of n that satisfies both [1] and [2] is 23.",
