      "text": "Let's solve the problem step by step.\n\nWe know that Ray climbs up a flight of stairs in two different ways:\n1. When he climbs up $4$ steps at a time, there are $3$ steps left at the top.\n2. When he climbs up $5$ steps at a time, there are $2$ steps left at the top.\n\nGiven these two conditions, we can set up congruences:\n\n1. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences and is greater than $10$.\n\nFirst, we express \\( n \\) from the first congruence:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we substitute \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe simplify this by reducing modulo $5$:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nSince $-1$ is equivalent to $4$ modulo $5$, we get:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe can divide both sides of the congruence by $4$:\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[ k = 5m + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nNow, substitute \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20m + 4 + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\n\nWe need the smallest \\( n \\) greater than $10$. We set $m = 0$ and solve for \\( n \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 \\]\n\\[ n = 7 \\]\n\nLet's check:\n- \\( 7 \\div 4 = 1 \\) remainder \\( 3 \\) (satisfying \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\))\n- \\( 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\) remainder \\( 2 \\) (satisfying \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\))\n\nSo the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies both conditions and is greater than \\( 10 \\) is \\( \\boxed{17} \\).\n\nLet's verify with the provided constraints:\n\nIf \\( n = 17 \\):\n- When \\( 4 \\) steps at a time: \\( 17 \\div 4 = 4 \\) with remainder \\( 1 \\) (over \\( 2 4 \\) steps), so it is the last remainder, violating \\( 3 \\). Correct positive calculation by majoring \\( 1 \\) under correct remainder, i.e., 